   "title": "Dollar to Naira exchange rate today, November 4, 2026",
   "url": "https://www.vanguardngr.com/2026/11/dollar-to-naira-exchange-rate-today-november-4-2026/",
   "domain": "vanguardngr.com",
   "source": "vanguardngr.com",
   "published": "Wed, 04 Nov 2026 06:35:57 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793774157.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "Layer 1 The dollar-to-naira exchange rate remains a major focus for businesses, importers and Nigerians seeking to meet their foreign exchange needs on Wednesday, November 4, 2026. The Nigerian naira trades against the US dollar at different rates across the Nigerian Foreign Exchange Market (NFEM) and the parallel market, also known as the black market. The latest reported official NFEM rate is approximately ₦1,346.43 per dollar, based on the November 3 market publication.",
   "summary_zh": "第一层 2026年11月4日星期三，美元兑奈拉汇率仍是企业、进口商以及希望满足外汇需求的尼日利亚人关注的重点。奈拉在尼日利亚外汇市场（NFEM）和平行市场（又称黑市）以不同汇率兑美元交易。根据11月3日的市场公布数据，NFEM最新官方汇率约为每美元₦1,346.43。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Layer 1 The dollar-to-naira exchange rate remains a major focus for businesses, importers and Nigerians seeking to meet their foreign exchange needs on Wednesday, November 4, 2026. The Nigerian naira trades against the US dollar at different rates across the Nigerian Foreign Exchange Market (NFEM) and the parallel market, also known as the black market. The NFEM rate reflects transactions in the official foreign exchange market, while the parallel market rate is influenced by demand and supply among independent currency dealers. As of Wednesday morning, the dollar is quoted at approximately ₦1,380 for buying and ₦1,390 for selling in the parallel market. The latest reported official NFEM rate is approximately ₦1,346.43 per dollar, based on the November 3 market publication. In the parallel market, Bureau De Change operators offer different rates depending on location, dollar availability and transaction volume. The quoted buying and selling rates are therefore indicative and may change throughout the day. At the prevailing rates, $100 is worth approximately ₦134,643 at the official market rate and ₦139,000 at the parallel-market selling rate. The difference between the official and parallel-market selling rates is approximately ₦43.57 per dollar. Meanwhile, the naira’s performance in the official market remains influenced by foreign exchange supply, demand from importers and other market participants, and liquidity conditions. Nigerians are advised to confirm prevailing rates with their banks and authorised Bureau De Change operators before conducting foreign exchange transactions, as rates may fluctuate during the day. Comments expressed here do not reflect the opinions of Vanguard newspapers or any employee thereof",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
