   "title": "US trade commission terminates Samsung chip patent probe following settlement with Netlist",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/business/companies/20261103/us-trade-commission-terminates-samsung-chip-patent-probe-following-settlement-with-netlist",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Tue, 03 Nov 2026 02:42:03 GMT",
   "_dt": 1793673723.0,
   "ageDays": 1.2,
   "summary": "WASHINGTON — The U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC) has formally terminated a patent infringement investigation involving Samsung Electronics Co. and several major American technology companies following a settlement between the Korean chipmaker and U.S. chip firm Netlist Inc., according to a federal notice published Monday. The case was launched in July following a complaint from Netlist, which alleged that certain memory products infringed its patents.",
   "summary_zh": "华盛顿——据周一发布的一份联邦通知，美国国际贸易委员会（USITC）已正式终止一项涉及三星电子有限公司和数家美国大型科技公司的专利侵权调查；此前，这家韩国芯片制造商与美国芯片企业Netlist Inc.已达成和解。此案于7月启动，源于Netlist的投诉。该公司指控某些存储产品侵犯了其专利。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "WASHINGTON — The U.S. International Trade Commission (USITC) has formally terminated a patent infringement investigation involving Samsung Electronics Co. and several major American technology companies following a settlement between the Korean chipmaker and U.S. chip firm Netlist Inc., according to a federal notice published Monday. The investigation centered on certain dynamic random access memory (DRAM) devices, products containing them and related components, according to the USITC notice published in the U.S. Federal Register. The case was launched in July following a complaint from Netlist, which alleged that certain memory products infringed its patents. Other companies named as respondents in the case included Google, Nvidia Corp., Broadcom Inc. and Super Micro Computer Inc. The notice said Netlist and Samsung, including its two U.S. subsidiaries, jointly requested the termination of the investigation on Aug. 18 based on a settlement agreement between the companies. In their August settlement, Samsung signed a five-year agreement with Netlist to access the American company's patent portfolio, including technologies related to server dual in-line memory modules (DIMMs) and high-bandwidth memory (HBM) used in artificial intelligence (AI) servers and high-performance computing systems. Netlist develops advanced memory and storage technologies, including server memory and HBM solutions for AI computing. In 2021, Netlist sued Samsung, alleging that the Korean tech giant's memory products used in cloud computing servers, along with other data technologies, infringed its patents. Samsung had argued that Netlist's patents were invalid and that its technologies operated differently from those covered by the patents",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "kr"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "eco"
   ],
   "tier": 2,
   "actor": "美国",
