   "title": "🚨 Chinese vessels suspected of surveying undersea cables have been spotted near Japan, Taiwan and the Philippines, all situated along the Fi",
   "url": "https://x.com/Byron_Wan/status/2116954840433881569",
   "snippet": "🚨 Chinese vessels suspected of surveying undersea cables have been spotted near Japan, Taiwan and the Philippines, all situated along the First Island Chain.\n\nStarting in late Sep, the Chinese research vessel Jia Hai Ke 7 engaged in survey activities for several days without Manila’s permission inside the exclusive economic zone around the Batanes Islands, the Philippine’s northernmost territory, which also faces Taiwan. Manila expressed deep concern, noting that vital undersea cables run through the area.\n\nAlthough the vessel is reportedly owned by a private Chinese firm, two China Coast Guard ships moved in to escort it when Philippine patrol vessels and aircraft approached. The survey was clearly not an independent operation.\n\nSurveillance data suggests similar activity near Japan. Analysis of tracking data from AIS found Chinese research vessels suspected of mapping undersea cables off Japan.\n\nSince 2024, research vessels affiliated with a Chinese university have repeatedly slowed, stopped and executed sharp turns in international waters east of Hokkaido and within Japan’s EEZ off Nagoya. These abnormal maneuvers directly overlapped with identified undersea cables.",
   "body": "🚨 Chinese vessels suspected of surveying undersea cables have been spotted near Japan, Taiwan and the Philippines, all situated along the First Island Chain.\n\nStarting in late Sep, the Chinese research vessel Jia Hai Ke 7 engaged in survey activities for several days without Manila’s permission inside the exclusive economic zone around the Batanes Islands, the Philippine’s northernmost territory, which also faces Taiwan. Manila expressed deep concern, noting that vital undersea cables run through the area.\n\nAlthough the vessel is reportedly owned by a private Chinese firm, two China Coast Guard ships moved in to escort it when Philippine patrol vessels and aircraft approached. The survey was clearly not an independent operation.\n\nSurveillance data suggests similar activity near Japan. Analysis of tracking data from AIS found Chinese research vessels suspected of mapping undersea cables off Japan.\n\nSince 2024, research vessels affiliated with a Chinese university have repeatedly slowed, stopped and executed sharp turns in international waters east of Hokkaido and within Japan’s EEZ off Nagoya. These abnormal maneuvers directly overlapped with identified undersea cables.",
